'''tremendous''' (trẹ̄-men′dus), ''a.'' [= Sp. Pg. It. ''tremendo'', < L. ''tremendus'', fearful, terrible, gerundive of ''tremere'', quake, tremble: see ''tremble''.] 1. Such as may or does excite trembling, fear, or awe; overpowering in character or quality; awful; dreadful: as, a ''tremendous'' explosion; ''tremendous'' invective.

{{smaller block|Secondly, [a precept] about blessing, or rather not blaspheming the ''tremendous'' name of God.
{{right|''Evelyn'', True Religion, II. 17.}}}}

{{smaller block|The battle of Ravenna, one of those ''tremendous'' days into which human folly and wickedness compress the whole devastation of a famine or a plague.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Machiavelli.}}}}

Hence — 2. Such as to excite astonishment or awe; unexampled; wonderful in a high degree; overwhelming; astounding: used intensively or hyperbolically.

{{smaller block|The floor of each story was arched, the walls of ''tremendous'' thickness.
{{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, xxvi.}}}}

{{smaller block|From the trees we sometimes saw hanging pythons of ''tremendous'' girth.
{{right|''P. Robinson'', Under the Sun, p. 130.}}}}

{{smaller block|The skilfullest crew that ever launched a life-boat would be dashed in pieces in a moment in those ''tremendous'' rollers.
{{right|''Froude'', Sketches, p. 198.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' 1. Frightful, terrific, horrible, appalling.}}

'''tremendously''' (trẹ̄-men′dus-li), ''adv.'' In a tremendous manner; in a manner to awe or astonish; with excessive force or magnitude.

'''tremendousness''' (trẹ̄-men′dus-nes), ''n.'' The state or property of being tremendous.

